  "intro": "Standalone drill set, not tied to any lesson. The Relativpronomen (der/die/das/die) matches the antecedent's GENDER and NUMBER, but its CASE comes only from its own job inside the relative clause — not from whatever article the antecedent had. This set deliberately uses \"ein/einen\" antecedents a lot, because \"einen Bruder, der ...\" (not \"den ...\") is the exact trap where learners copy the antecedent's case by mistake. Blocks: A Basistraining → B Vertiefung → C Sprachbausteine → D Wiederholung. Answers in solutions.md.\n",
  "lesson": "EX/04-relativsatz-a2",
  "level": "A2",
  "summary": null,
  "title": "Relativsätze: Nominativ und Akkusativ — A2",
  "topic": "Relativsätze und Relativpronomen"
